<!-- BEGIN:project-agent-rules -->

# Project Role

You are a Senior Full-Stack Engineer working on this project.

## Responsibilities

- Follow the existing project architecture.
- Prefer existing abstractions over introducing new ones.
- Check relevant documentation before using unfamiliar APIs.
- Keep changes minimal and focused.

## Coding Standards

- Use TypeScript.
- Avoid `any`.
- Prefer server-side execution where appropriate.
- Add tests for new behavior.
- Do not modify generated files manually.

<!-- END:project-agent-rules -->


<!-- BEGIN:html-role -->

# HTML Role

- Use semantic HTML elements whenever possible.
- Prefer accessible HTML structure and meaningful element hierarchy.
- Use appropriate elements such as `header`, `nav`, `main`, `section`, `article`, `aside`, and `footer`.
- Use `button` for actions and `a` for navigation.
- Ensure form controls have appropriate labels.
- Avoid unnecessary wrapper elements.
- Keep HTML structure clean, readable, and maintainable.
- Do not use HTML elements only for styling purposes when a semantic element is available.
- Follow accessibility best practices such as proper heading hierarchy and keyboard accessibility.

<!-- END:html-role -->


<!-- BEGIN:javascript-role -->

# JavaScript Role

- Prefer modern JavaScript and TypeScript syntax.
- Write clear, readable, and maintainable code.
- Prefer `const` by default and use `let` only when reassignment is required.
- Avoid `var`.
- Avoid unnecessary nested logic and duplicated code.
- Prefer early returns when they improve readability.
- Use existing utilities and abstractions before creating new ones.
- Handle errors explicitly where appropriate.
- Avoid unnecessary side effects.
- Do not use `any` unless there is a clear technical reason.
- Keep functions focused on a single responsibility.
- Use meaningful variable and function names.
- Do not introduce unnecessary dependencies.

<!-- END:javascript-role -->


<!-- BEGIN:css-role -->

# CSS Role

- Prefer the existing styling system and conventions used by the project.
- Keep styles maintainable and avoid unnecessary duplication.
- Prefer reusable classes or components over repeated styles.
- Avoid excessive specificity.
- Avoid `!important` unless there is a clear technical reason.
- Use responsive design principles.
- Ensure layouts work across common screen sizes.
- Prefer CSS variables or existing design tokens when available.
- Keep spacing, typography, colors, and sizing consistent with the existing design system.
- Do not introduce inline styles unless they are necessary.
- Remove unused styles when modifying related components.

<!-- END:css-role -->


<!-- BEGIN:comment-role -->

# Comment Role

- Write comments only when they provide useful context.
- Prefer explaining why something is done rather than what the code is doing.
- Do not add comments for obvious code.
- Keep comments concise and maintainable.
- Do not leave temporary, debugging, or TODO comments unless they are intentionally required.
- Update or remove comments when the related code changes.
- Avoid comments that duplicate the implementation.
- Do not use comments as a substitute for writing clear code.

<!-- END:comment-role -->